[2026-10-28 18:26:10] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment (current bath: 10 wt% PolarClean)

The current campaign has introduced the 10 wt% PolarClean bath. I need to identify the non-dominated frontier across **both** objectives (Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction) using only experiments with valid measurements for both.

Key observations from experiments with the 10 wt% PolarClean bath (the current context):

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 15-0add-9deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s | 124.3 | 0.55 | Good |
| 14.5-0add-10deg-PolarClean-10wt-200s | 132.7 | 0.62 | Good |
| 14.5-0add-10deg-PolarClean-10wt-200s_run2 | 105.5 | 0.66 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-9deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s_run2 | 110.7 | 0.59 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-6deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s | 159.0 | 0.61 | Minor crease |
| 15.5-0add-6deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s_run2 | 162.8 | 0.73 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-6deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s_run3 | 215.0 | 0.57 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-6deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s_run4 | 151.1 | 0.82 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-8deg-PolarClean-10wt-350s | 135.6 | 0.57 | Good |
| 15.5-0add-5deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s | 191.5 | 0.62 | Good |

The 10 wt% PolarClean bath clearly reduces modulus compared to 0 wt% bath experiments at similar polymer concentrations, but appears to support higher pore fractions. The best two-objective candidates in the 10 wt% bath are:
- **15.5 wt%, 6°C bath, 250s wait, run4**: Modulus 151, Pore Fraction **0.82** — highest pore fraction seen in this bath
- **15.5 wt%, 6°C bath, 250s wait, run3**: Modulus **215**, Pore Fraction 0.57 — highest modulus in this bath
- **15.5 wt%, 5°C bath, 250s wait**: Modulus 192, Pore Fraction 0.62 — balanced

The 15.5 wt% / 6°C / 250s condition (runs 1–4) shows high variability in pore fraction (0.57–0.82) and modulus (151–215), suggesting the process is sensitive and not yet reproducibly characterized. Run 4 (PF=0.82) is a single observation with no independent repeat confirming that high pore fraction. Run 3 (modulus=215) is also a single observation.

### Validation Policy Check

The condition **polymer_wt=15.5, bath_temp=6, pullcast_speed=6, mixing_temp=40, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=250, nips_bath_wait_time=1600, nitrogen=True, nips_bath_solvent=PolarClean 10wt%** has 4 runs with large spread (PF: 0.57–0.82, modulus: 151–215). This is a Pareto-front candidate in the current bath context with high CV and uncertain performance. However, 4 runs already exist — the spread itself is informative and suggests genuine process variability rather than a need for more repeats at this exact point.

### Exploration Decision

Given:
1. The 10 wt% PolarClean bath is new and under-explored
2. The 15.5 wt% / 6°C / 250s cluster shows high pore fractions but moderate modulus
3. We want to explore whether **higher polymer concentration** (e.g., 16–16.5 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath and cold bath temperature can push modulus up while maintaining reasonable pore fraction — a region not yet tested in this bath
4. The 15.5 wt% / 5°C / 250s run showed modulus=192, PF=0.62 with good quality — stepping to 16 wt% at 5°C is a natural Pareto-improvement probe
5. The 16 wt% / 5°C condition in the 10 wt% bath was tried once (modulus=223, PF=0.52) but had a top-gap defect — a nearby but distinct point (different wait time or speed) is worth exploring

**Proposed experiment**: polymer_wt=16.0, bath_temp=5°C, mixing_temp=45°C, pullcast_speed=5 mm/s (slower than the 6 mm/s used before to reduce coverage defects), coupon_to_bath_wait_time=300s (slightly longer than 250s to allow better film formation), nips_bath_wait_time=1600s, nitrogen=True.

This is distinct from the prior 16-0add-5deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s experiment (which used speed=6, wait=250s and had a top-gap defect). Reducing speed to 5 mm/s and increasing wait to 300s may improve coverage while probing whether higher polymer concentration in the 10 wt% bath can improve modulus without sacrificing pore fraction relative to the 15.5 wt% cluster.

**Expected benefit**: If higher polymer concentration raises modulus (as seen in 0 wt% bath experiments) while the 10 wt% bath maintains elevated pore fraction, this could be a Pareto improvement over the 15.5 wt% / 10 wt% bath results. The slower cast speed should reduce coverage defects seen at speed=6.

